{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794649651,"nonce":"Q7ZIf1lN2a+dMSekxbzk1faO9I4=","title":"The iOS and Android runners never turn motion promotion off, so a phone bakes every animated node to a texture","message":"`BlincApp::from_context`, which both mobile runners build the app with, did not apply `BlincConfig::motion_promotion` to the render context. The context's own default is on, so on a phone bound nodes and CSS animations were baked into a texture each and moved by blitting. The rows paths (CSS transform and opacity animations, FLIP slides, scroll rows) are used only where promotion is off, so none of them has run on a phone. The desktop constructors do apply the configuration, which is why the desktop windows and the headless harness never showed it. `from_context` now applies it, and `a_context_takes_the_motion_promotion_of_its_configuration` fails without that line.\n\nFound while checking the runner gate in efa5528 on the iPhone. A Time Profiler run of the build with the gate change alone put 6.7 s of the main thread's 7.3 s in `blinc_render_frame` under `composite_css_layers_overlay`, in `blit_tight_texture_to_target`: one blit per animated card per frame, which is also what the Metal trace in d20f077 showed.\n\niPhone 12 Pro Max, mobile/example release build, scene `cards_plain` (200 childless cards under a CSS keyframe animation of transform and opacity). fps, render median and footprint are from the BLINC_BENCH_STATS windows of 120 frames; CPU is the slope of the cumulative on-core time in an xctrace Activity Monitor recording of a running process over 10 s, all threads.\n- Before (promotion on, the runner raising a full paint on any CSS activity): 36.0 fps, render 18 ms, 620 to 714 MB, 1.95 cores.\n- Runner gate only (efa5528): 39.8 fps, render 15.5 to 16.5 ms, 656 to 708 MB, 2.21 cores.\n- Gate and this fix: 59 fps, render 3.2 ms, 145 MB, 0.53 cores.\n\n`cards` (a text child on each card, so the opacity cannot be carried and the cards are painted in full), before against after:\n- 20 cards: 40.5 and 40.2 fps, 483 and 483 MB, render 6.70 and 6.64 ms.\n- 60 cards: 29.9 and 29.7 fps, 1192 and 1089 MB, render 18.1 and 20.6 ms.\n- 100 cards: 19.6 and 20.0 fps, 1660 and 1278 MB, render 36.1 and 40.5 ms.\n- 200 cards: killed within 2 s before and after (d20f077).\nThe render median is 12 to 14% higher at 60 and 100 cards for a reason I have not found, with the frame rate unchanged.\n\nOne run of each on one device. Android builds its app the same way and takes the same fix; it has not been run on a device. The screen was not captured on the phone, so how the animated cards look there has not been compared with the desktop.\n\nRelated: efa5528, d20f077.","files":null}]}